00:12:35,069 --> 00:12:37,655
Yeah, for one summer at sad camp.

262
00:12:40,200 --> 00:12:41,701
He might not even remember me.

263
00:12:41,701 --> 00:12:43,912
He'll remember you. That shit bonds you for life.

264
00:12:44,954 --> 00:12:46,748
And you're memorable.

265
00:12:46,748 --> 00:12:48,166
Even if he does,

266
00:12:48,166 --> 00:12:50,418
it doesn't mean I know how to find his missing dad.

267
00:12:50,418 --> 00:12:53,379
Okay, but it gives us a leg up on everyone else.

268
00:12:53,379 --> 00:12:57,759
We are not people to turn our noses up at a hundred grand, Holmsey.

269
00:12:57,759 --> 00:13:02,305
I am currently washing ball pit balls for $8.40 an hour.

270
00:13:02,305 --> 00:13:04,557
Do you know what I'm washing off the balls?

271
00:13:04,557 --> 00:13:05,558
Do you?

272
00:13:06,851 --> 00:13:08,811
- It's pee. - Mmm.

273
00:13:08,811 --> 00:13:12,482
It's pee, Aza. Pee-pee.

274
00:13:12,482 --> 00:13:13,650
Yeah, okay.

275
00:13:13,650 --> 00:13:15,568
And as much as I would love to think

276
00:13:15,568 --> 00:13:17,487
that my Star Wars fanfic is going to make me stupid rich,

277
00:13:17,487 --> 00:13:19,405
just can't rely on that right now.

278
00:13:19,405 --> 00:13:22,242
Ergo, we owe it to ourselves,

279
00:13:22,242 --> 00:13:26,788
to our families, and to our futures to try and get this 100 grand.

280
00:13:28,957 --> 00:13:31,376
That kind of money, we could order sodas.

281
00:13:31,376 --> 00:13:33,336
[curious music playing]

282
00:13:33,336 --> 00:13:35,547
I just don't get how Russell Pickett disappeared without anyone knowing.

283
00:13:35,547 --> 00:13:38,508
I mean, don't rich people have a bunch of security cameras

284
00:13:38,508 --> 00:13:40,009
in their manors?

285
00:13:40,009 --> 00:13:44,806
Like, to make sure that nobody steals their jewels?

286
00:13:44,806 --> 00:13:46,975
Actually, they only have one camera.

287
00:13:47,684 --> 00:13:49,352
How do you know?

288
00:13:49,352 --> 00:13:51,854
After that summer at camp, Davis invited me to his 13th birthday.

289
00:13:51,854 --> 00:13:54,065
Some kid got him this motion capture camera

290
00:13:54,065 --> 00:13:55,608
because he was really into tracking animals,

291
00:13:55,608 --> 00:13:57,277
and in front of everyone,

292
00:13:57,277 --> 00:13:59,362
his dad just turned all cold and said he couldn't keep it.

293
00:13:59,362 --> 00:14:00,530
Why?

294
00:14:00,530 --> 00:14:02,448
Uh, because...

295
00:14:02,448 --> 00:14:04,617
[imitating Russell] "Privacy is the most important thing you can have,

296
00:14:04,617 --> 00:14:06,911
and if you don't have that, you have nothing."

297
00:14:06,911 --> 00:14:08,913
[scoffs]

298
00:14:08,913 --> 00:14:11,457
It's a pretty crazy speech to give a kid who's just trying to enjoy his birthday

299
00:14:11,457 --> 00:14:14,460
and not be sad about his dead mom.

300
00:14:14,460 --> 00:14:15,670
Seriously.

301
00:14:15,670 --> 00:14:17,297
Anyway, uh, Davis got upset,

302
00:14:17,297 --> 00:14:20,758
and, finally, his dad caved and let him keep it.

303
00:14:20,758 --> 00:14:21,843
Huh.

304
00:14:21,843 --> 00:14:23,428
No, no, no, don't...

305
00:14:23,428 --> 00:14:24,596
- Don't lean. - [music stops]

306
00:14:24,596 --> 00:14:26,347
Sorry. Sorry.

307
00:14:27,890 --> 00:14:29,809
Okay. So...

308
00:14:29,809 --> 00:14:33,396
we've gotta get our hands on that camera. Do you know where he installed it?

309
00:14:33,896 --> 00:14:35,982
Sort of.

310
00:14:35,982 --> 00:14:39,068
We all helped him put it up in this huge tree near the river.

311
00:14:39,902 --> 00:14:43,156
Holmsey, we have a lead.

312
00:14:44,616 --> 00:14:46,993
- [cell phone clicks] - [typing]

313
00:14:46,993 --> 00:14:49,912
- [whispers] What are you doing? - I'm on the phone.

314
00:14:49,912 --> 00:14:52,665
Eric, hi. It's Daisy.

315
00:14:52,665 --> 00:14:54,334
Yes, that's... that's actually why I'm calling.

316
00:14:54,334 --> 00:14:56,836
I really, really need the afternoon off.

317
00:14:56,836 --> 00:14:59,839
I'm pretty sure that I have...

318
00:14:59,839 --> 00:15:03,468
[quietly] What's a highly contagious stomach bug?

319
00:15:04,302 --> 00:15:05,470
Norovirus.

320
00:15:05,470 --> 00:15:06,888
[in normal voice] Definitely Norovirus.

321
00:15:06,888 --> 00:15:08,890
100% it's Norovirus.

322
00:15:08,890 --> 00:15:11,768
It feels like Norovirus.

323
00:15:13,394 --> 00:15:14,520
["Fig in Leather" instrumental playing]

324
00:15:14,520 --> 00:15:16,522
Thank you. Okay.

325
00:15:16,522 --> 00:15:17,899
- Bye. - [cell phone clicks]

326
00:15:20,318 --> 00:15:22,070
Do you still have that canoe?

327
00:15:33,915 --> 00:15:36,417
This water has to be like, 50% urine.

328
00:15:36,417 --> 00:15:38,086
That's the good half.

329
00:15:42,507 --> 00:15:43,800
Kidding.

330
00:15:43,800 --> 00:15:45,718
[Aza] This water is teeming with bacteria.

331
00:15:47,637 --> 00:15:49,097
[Daisy] What a day!

332
00:15:49,097 --> 00:15:54,811
The sun's a-shining, the canoe's a-floating,

333
00:15:54,811 --> 00:15:57,647
and we're about to live the American dream.

334
00:15:57,647 --> 00:16:01,484
Benefiting from someone else's misfortune.

335
00:16:01,484 --> 00:16:04,195
Could you please row a little gentler, so I don't get splashed?

336
00:16:04,195 --> 00:16:05,405
Sorry.

337
00:16:06,155 --> 00:16:07,365
[static crackles]

338
00:16:07,365 --> 00:16:09,033
[Aza] The water will get on you,

339
00:16:09,033 --> 00:16:10,743
and then it's only a matter of time before--

340
00:16:10,743 --> 00:16:12,578
Hey, did you know that this river is the only reason

341
00:16:12,578 --> 00:16:14,247
that Indianapolis even exists?

342
00:16:14,247 --> 00:16:16,499
I know you're trying to distract me.

343
00:16:16,499 --> 00:16:19,460
Yep, but did you know my thing?

344
00:16:19,460 --> 00:16:22,171
[Aza] Bacteria can lead to Giardia.

345
00:16:22,171 --> 00:16:26,259
So, back when Indiana first became a state,

346
00:16:26,259 --> 00:16:29,804
there were all of these heated debates about where the capital should be.

347
00:16:29,804 --> 00:16:33,933
And they noticed that there's a river right smack in the center of the state.

348
00:16:33,933 --> 00:16:38,146
So, they're like, "Boomshakalaka, perfect place for our capital."

349
00:16:38,146 --> 00:16:40,690
But then, after they made the announcement,

350
00:16:40,690 --> 00:16:44,193
they realized that the White River is, like, six inches deep,

351
00:16:44,193 --> 00:16:47,572
and there's no way in hell you can float a steamship down it.

352
00:16:48,448 --> 00:16:49,699
For a long time,

353
00:16:49,699 --> 00:16:51,617
Indianapolis was the largest city in the world

354
00:16:51,617 --> 00:16:54,036
not on a navigable waterway.

355
00:16:54,036 --> 00:16:55,830
That's actually really interesting.

356
00:16:55,830 --> 00:16:57,665
Yeah, I knew you'd like that.

357
00:16:59,584 --> 00:17:01,753
And look at that, we're here!

358
00:17:03,045 --> 00:17:04,130
Almost!

359
00:17:04,130 --> 00:17:05,089
[music fades]

360
00:17:05,089 --> 00:17:06,174
[grunts]

361
00:17:08,217 --> 00:17:11,179
Maybe this is a literal sign we should go home now.

362
00:17:12,096 --> 00:17:13,097
Nah.

363
00:17:16,392 --> 00:17:18,686
It's plausible deniability.

364
00:17:18,686 --> 00:17:21,397
Now... [sighs] which way to that camera, Holmsey?

365
00:17:22,732 --> 00:17:24,233
I have no idea.

366
00:17:25,193 --> 00:17:26,652
[sighs in exasperation]

367
00:17:26,652 --> 00:17:28,738
Why can't you have that super observant kind of OCD

368
00:17:28,738 --> 00:17:30,990
that makes you an amazing detective

369
00:17:30,990 --> 00:17:33,659
in all those crime shows that my dad watches?

370
00:17:33,659 --> 00:17:35,745
["Dance Yrself Clean" playing]

371
00:17:39,457 --> 00:17:41,375
[Daisy] Shh!

372
00:17:41,375 --> 00:17:42,835
[imitating lightsaber]

373
00:17:45,838 --> 00:17:46,756
[imitating hacking]

374
00:17:55,223 --> 00:17:56,724
Does this look familiar at all?

375
00:17:56,724 --> 00:17:59,227
♪ Walking up to me Expecting... ♪

376
00:17:59,227 --> 00:18:01,813
No? Just woods? Okay.

377
00:18:01,813 --> 00:18:04,065
[Daisy imitating lightsaber]

378
00:18:04,065 --> 00:18:05,525
What are you doing?

379
00:18:07,777 --> 00:18:08,736
Lightsaber.

380
00:18:09,946 --> 00:18:11,656
[clicks tongue] You try.

381
00:18:13,991 --> 00:18:15,243
You got it.

382
00:18:15,952 --> 00:18:18,496
♪ Ah ♪

383
00:18:19,497 --> 00:18:21,999
Wingardium Leviosa.

384
00:18:25,253 --> 00:18:31,133
No. Wrong franchise. And I know that you know that.

385
00:18:31,133 --> 00:18:34,637
[sighs] Can we please just go home?

386
00:18:36,097 --> 00:18:39,141
This was an idiotic, massive waste of...

387
00:18:39,141 --> 00:18:40,142
[music stops]

388
00:18:41,811 --> 00:18:43,396
There's... there's the tree.

389
00:18:45,898 --> 00:18:47,149
And the camera.

390
00:18:48,025 --> 00:18:48,901
Yes!

391
00:18:48,901 --> 00:18:50,236
[exhales]

392
00:18:50,236 --> 00:18:51,863
What is happening up there?

393
00:18:51,863 --> 00:18:53,990
I'm not exactly known for my core strength.

394
00:18:53,990 --> 00:18:55,783
It won't sync with my phone.

395
00:18:55,783 --> 00:18:57,076
Well, is your Bluetooth on?

396
00:18:57,076 --> 00:18:58,828
Yes, my Bluetooth is on.

397
00:19:00,997 --> 00:19:02,373
- Oh. - What?

398
00:19:02,373 --> 00:19:04,375
- It wasn't on. - [clicks tongue] Aza!

399
00:19:05,877 --> 00:19:07,128
- [cell phone dings] - [Aza gasps]

400
00:19:07,128 --> 00:19:08,838
- Okay. It's working. - Yeah?

401
00:19:08,838 --> 00:19:11,132
- Yeah, the photos are loading. - Okay.

402
00:19:11,132 --> 00:19:13,092
Um, I don't want to alarm you,

403
00:19:13,092 --> 00:19:15,970
but there is definitely a golf cart headed in our vague direction.

404
00:19:15,970 --> 00:19:18,139
Wait. [sputtering] What do you mean vague?

405
00:19:18,139 --> 00:19:19,891
All right, I think it's just making the rounds.

406
00:19:19,891 --> 00:19:21,225
We're good. Relax.

407
00:19:22,602 --> 00:19:25,730
Nope, not good. Very bad. It definitely saw us.

408
00:19:25,730 --> 00:19:27,148
- One sec, one sec, one sec. - Come on, hurry!

409
00:19:27,148 --> 00:19:28,816
- Okay, okay, okay. - Hurry! Good? Good?

410
00:19:28,816 --> 00:19:30,026
- Yep, yep, yep. Mm-hmm. - We're good? We're good? Okay.

411
00:19:30,026 --> 00:19:31,360
Okay. We're good. Okay, okay.

412
00:19:31,360 --> 00:19:32,862
- [Aza breathing heavily] - Ah!

413
00:19:32,862 --> 00:19:34,780
- What do we do? - [panting]

414
00:19:34,780 --> 00:19:36,449
We "damsel in distress."

415
00:19:36,449 --> 00:19:38,075
- Yeah, um... - [siren blaring faintly]

416
00:19:40,286 --> 00:19:41,954
Oh, no, I'm not doing that. I'm not doing that.

417
00:19:41,954 --> 00:19:44,040
Okay, all right. That's fine. That's fine, don't worry.

418
00:19:44,040 --> 00:19:45,458
God.

419
00:19:45,458 --> 00:19:47,251
[Daisy] There. There. There.

420
00:19:48,252 --> 00:19:49,503
No!

421
00:19:49,503 --> 00:19:51,422
- What are you two doing here? - [Daisy pants]

422
00:19:51,422 --> 00:19:53,299
Other than trespassing?

423
00:19:54,717 --> 00:19:58,012
Hi. Thank God. We, um...

424
00:19:58,012 --> 00:20:01,307
We were canoeing and we must have hit a rock.

425
00:20:02,391 --> 00:20:03,476
Multiple rocks.

426
00:20:03,476 --> 00:20:05,686
We started taking on water.

427
00:20:05,686 --> 00:20:08,314
[clears throat, coughs]

428
00:20:08,314 --> 00:20:09,941
Barely made it to shore.

429
00:20:09,941 --> 00:20:12,443
But Aza here,

430
00:20:12,443 --> 00:20:16,030
she actually happens to be friends with Davis Pickett.

431
00:20:16,030 --> 00:20:17,281
Maybe you could take us to him.

432
00:20:21,077 --> 00:20:22,495
It would be my pleasure.

433
00:20:23,746 --> 00:20:24,580
Oh.

434
00:20:25,748 --> 00:20:27,458
Thank you.

435
00:20:27,458 --> 00:20:30,586
Because if he doesn't know your friend, I'm calling the police.

436
00:20:31,879 --> 00:20:33,923
Fun.

437
00:20:33,923 --> 00:20:37,343
Sad camp does bond you for life, doesn't it?

438
00:20:37,343 --> 00:20:39,595
["The Less I Know The Better" playing]

439
00:20:44,016 --> 00:20:46,060
[Daisy] Whoo! Bumpy.

440
00:20:51,023 --> 00:20:52,233
[door opens]

441
00:20:57,655 --> 00:20:59,031
[Lyle clears throat]

442
00:20:59,031 --> 00:21:00,241
[door closes]

443
00:21:00,241 --> 00:21:03,202
Davis, you have, uh, some visitors.

444
00:21:03,202 --> 00:21:04,870
[song continues on speakers]

445
00:21:04,870 --> 00:21:07,415
Aza. What are you doing here?

446
00:21:08,290 --> 00:21:10,376
Hi, Davis.

447
00:21:10,376 --> 00:21:13,254
See, I told you they knew each other.

448
00:21:13,254 --> 00:21:19,468
Uh, sorry, we hit a rock and washed ashore on your... mansion.

449
00:21:19,468 --> 00:21:21,971
Uh, apologies for the intrusion.

450
00:21:25,516 --> 00:21:28,102
Uh, well, I'm gonna... I think I'm gonna...

451
00:21:28,102 --> 00:21:29,979
I'm gonna go make a phone call.

452
00:21:29,979 --> 00:21:33,232
Um, make sure that we have that canoe insurance

453
00:21:33,232 --> 00:21:35,151
that I was telling you about.

454
00:21:35,818 --> 00:21:36,944
State Farm?

455
00:21:39,780 --> 00:21:42,908
Sorry about Daisy. Um...

456
00:21:42,908 --> 00:21:44,243
She's a...

457
00:21:46,078 --> 00:21:47,705
Leo.

458
00:21:47,705 --> 00:21:49,582
You know what's crazy?

459
00:21:49,582 --> 00:21:51,709
I was just thinking about you.

460
00:21:51,709 --> 00:21:53,794
You were?

461
00:21:53,794 --> 00:21:56,547
Yeah, everyone on the news keeps using my dad's full name,

462
00:21:56,547 --> 00:21:57,798
Russell Davis Pickett.

463
00:21:57,798 --> 00:22:00,718
And I keep thinking, "That's my name."

464
00:22:02,511 --> 00:22:04,221
That made you think of me?

465
00:22:04,221 --> 00:22:06,015
I remember you said that your dad wanted you to have

466
00:22:06,015 --> 00:22:07,933
your own unique name.

467
00:22:07,933 --> 00:22:09,560
A sound you could make your own.

468
00:22:10,352 --> 00:22:12,688
"A to Z and back again."

469
00:22:12,688 --> 00:22:14,356
My dad made me a junior.

470
00:22:15,357 --> 00:22:16,650
As I made juniority.

471
00:22:18,194 --> 00:22:21,322
Well, you're not your name.

472
00:22:23,741 --> 00:22:25,993
I'm really sorry about everything.

473
00:22:29,038 --> 00:22:30,956
I'm sure he'll turn up soon.

474
00:22:37,838 --> 00:22:39,965
I mean, I hope so, for Noah's sake,

475
00:22:41,258 --> 00:22:43,177
[sighs] but my dad's a shitbag.

476
00:22:44,220 --> 00:22:45,679
Maybe that's the wrong word.

477
00:22:45,679 --> 00:22:48,432
He's a coward and a criminal.

478
00:22:48,432 --> 00:22:52,269
He's probably holed up in the Swiss Alps, getting a massage right now.

479
00:22:54,230 --> 00:22:55,815
Hey, wanna play the next one with me?

480
00:22:55,815 --> 00:22:58,776
Sure, but first, say "hi." Remember my friend Aza?

481
00:22:58,776 --> 00:22:59,985
[Noah] Sup?

482
00:22:59,985 --> 00:23:02,196
Hey, nice to see you.

483
00:23:05,324 --> 00:23:07,493
Uh, well, I should get going. [chuckles]

484
00:23:07,493 --> 00:23:11,247
Do you need a ride home? 'Cause Lyle can take you. It's no problem.

485
00:23:11,247 --> 00:23:14,500
Sure. Thanks. [chuckles]

486
00:23:14,500 --> 00:23:17,711
I hope it's not years before I see you again, Aza.

487
00:23:22,883 --> 00:23:24,343
[song gets louder]

488
00:23:24,343 --> 00:23:28,973
♪ So, come on, Superman Say your stupid line ♪

489
00:23:31,016 --> 00:23:31,892
[song stops]

490
00:23:31,892 --> 00:23:34,645
- [sighs] Raccoon. - Raccoon.

491
00:23:34,645 --> 00:23:36,272
- Coyote. - Coyote.

492
00:23:36,272 --> 00:23:38,440
Are you gonna keep repeating me or...

493
00:23:38,440 --> 00:23:41,318
I can stop if it doesn't help.

494
00:23:41,318 --> 00:23:43,320
- [Aza] Raccoon. - [Daisy] Raccoon.

495
00:23:43,320 --> 00:23:44,780
- [Aza] Nothing. - [Daisy] Nothing.

496
00:23:47,158 --> 00:23:48,659
I'll stop. I'm done.

497
00:23:49,743 --> 00:23:51,245
- Nothing. - [inhales]

498
00:23:52,413 --> 00:23:54,206
- Nothing. - [groans loudly]

499
00:23:54,206 --> 00:23:55,791
It's taking too long.

500
00:23:57,001 --> 00:23:58,711
[Aza] Coyote.

501
00:23:58,711 --> 00:24:00,379
Ew! Coyote eating a raccoon.

502
00:24:00,379 --> 00:24:01,589
- Ew. - Oh.

503
00:24:02,590 --> 00:24:04,049
Look, there! That's him.

504
00:24:04,049 --> 00:24:07,386
That's literally him. Oh, my God! Oh, my God! [gasps]

505
00:24:07,386 --> 00:24:11,307
Oh! He's escaping in Burberry because, of course.

506
00:24:11,307 --> 00:24:13,434
Oh, my God, this is huge. This is huge.

507
00:24:13,434 --> 00:24:17,146
Okay, okay, um, we know that he left in the middle of the night

508
00:24:17,146 --> 00:24:20,816
because of the time stamp, and he brought a duffel bag.

509
00:24:22,568 --> 00:24:25,154
I'm not sure what this gets us.

510
00:24:26,071 --> 00:24:27,198
Yeah, me neither.

511
00:24:27,990 --> 00:24:29,241
But it's something!

512
00:24:29,241 --> 00:24:31,452
And lucky for us, Davis is totally into you.

513
00:24:31,452 --> 00:24:35,331
So, we'll just hang out with him again and get inside the belly of the beast.

514
00:24:35,331 --> 00:24:38,417
The mansion is the belly. Or is it the beast?

515
00:24:38,417 --> 00:24:41,378
I don't know. I don't... I've never understood that saying.

516
00:24:41,378 --> 00:24:42,546
Daisy...

517
00:24:44,590 --> 00:24:46,634
- you really think he's into me? - [scoffs]

518
00:24:48,052 --> 00:24:51,347
Yes, and you're into him, too.

519
00:24:51,347 --> 00:24:53,849
