772
00:37:56,881 --> 00:37:59,551
The parasite was in charge all along.

773
00:38:03,304 --> 00:38:05,515
Sometimes I feel like a fish.

774
00:38:08,143 --> 00:38:09,853
Not in control of my body.

775
00:38:11,479 --> 00:38:13,023
Not in control of my thoughts.

776
00:38:15,442 --> 00:38:17,027
I like the way your brain works.

777
00:38:18,903 --> 00:38:19,904
It hums.

778
00:38:21,573 --> 00:38:22,949
Where'd you learn that?

779
00:38:24,826 --> 00:38:28,621
This philosophy professor, Lucia Abbott.

780
00:38:28,621 --> 00:38:31,041
I've watched her TED talk, like, a dozen times.

781
00:38:32,709 --> 00:38:34,461
She teaches at Northwestern.

782
00:38:36,212 --> 00:38:40,967
I have this stupid dream of going there and taking her class.

783
00:38:40,967 --> 00:38:42,510
Why is that a stupid dream?

784
00:38:44,846 --> 00:38:49,309
[chuckling] I mean, it's insanely expensive.

785
00:38:49,309 --> 00:38:52,479
And then there's the whole living in Chicago part,

786
00:38:52,479 --> 00:38:55,899
which... would be new for me.

787
00:38:57,358 --> 00:38:59,944
New things aren't exactly my strong suit.

788
00:38:59,944 --> 00:39:03,114
Well, I'm sort of a new thing,

789
00:39:05,700 --> 00:39:07,077
and you're doin' fine with me.

790
00:39:08,453 --> 00:39:09,662
You're old-new.

791
00:39:11,539 --> 00:39:12,874
It's different.

792
00:39:14,375 --> 00:39:15,335
"Old-new."

793
00:39:17,545 --> 00:39:18,588
I like that.

794
00:39:22,092 --> 00:39:26,304
[Aza] If you kiss him, you'll get his bacteria in your mouth.

795
00:39:26,304 --> 00:39:28,431
His bacteria will make you sick.

796
00:39:29,808 --> 00:39:31,392
What are you thinking?

797
00:39:35,271 --> 00:39:36,606
You really wanna know?

798
00:39:37,732 --> 00:39:38,858
I do. [laughs]

799
00:39:40,902 --> 00:39:41,861
Okay.

800
00:39:42,821 --> 00:39:43,822
I was thinking...

801
00:39:45,698 --> 00:39:47,575
"I hope she's not after the reward."

802
00:39:51,579 --> 00:39:52,914
Davis, I'm not.

803
00:39:52,914 --> 00:39:54,332
[music fades]

804
00:39:54,332 --> 00:39:56,376
- I swear. - Aza.

805
00:39:56,376 --> 00:39:59,504
I know why you guys were in the woods the other day.

806
00:39:59,504 --> 00:40:01,339
You were looking for the camera, right?

807
00:40:03,091 --> 00:40:05,343
I checked it, too.

808
00:40:05,343 --> 00:40:07,971
Got a photo of him leaving with a duffel bag.

809
00:40:10,473 --> 00:40:13,935
I never actually cared about the reward.

810
00:40:15,478 --> 00:40:16,980
Daisy and I were just--

811
00:40:16,980 --> 00:40:17,939
I just realized...

812
00:40:19,941 --> 00:40:21,985
this is an entirely solvable problem.

813
00:40:22,986 --> 00:40:25,071
[funky music playing]

814
00:40:27,115 --> 00:40:29,033
[plastic rustling]

815
00:40:30,493 --> 00:40:31,619
What are you doing?

816
00:40:32,454 --> 00:40:34,539
[Davis] One second.

817
00:40:34,539 --> 00:40:36,749
- Are you into lizards? - Hmm?

818
00:40:37,584 --> 00:40:38,918
Oh, no, my dad is.

819
00:40:38,918 --> 00:40:43,047
Although, that is not a lizard. That's a tuatara.

820
00:40:43,965 --> 00:40:45,425
What is that?

821
00:40:45,425 --> 00:40:49,345
I don't know, some leftover from 200 million years ago

822
00:40:49,345 --> 00:40:51,764
that my dad believes holds the secret to longevity.

823
00:40:52,974 --> 00:40:54,309
You can learn all about it

824
00:40:54,309 --> 00:40:56,227
at the Pickett Center for Tuatara Research,

825
00:40:56,227 --> 00:40:58,855
which is a real thing that he's leaving most of his estate to.

826
00:40:58,855 --> 00:41:01,441
- That is insane. - Do you know what's worse?

827
00:41:01,441 --> 00:41:03,943
The money doesn't even go to taking care of the damn thing.

828
00:41:03,943 --> 00:41:07,363
It pays for studying its cells with all sorts of horrible experiments.

829
00:41:07,363 --> 00:41:10,033
- [footsteps retreating] - [drawer opens]

830
00:41:10,033 --> 00:41:10,992
[box rattles]

831
00:41:12,827 --> 00:41:16,289
This is for you.

832
00:41:17,749 --> 00:41:18,583
Oh.

833
00:41:20,668 --> 00:41:21,961
Thank you.

834
00:41:21,961 --> 00:41:23,880
Promise me you won't open it till you get home.

835
00:41:23,880 --> 00:41:28,092
And if you still wanna see me, give me a call.

836
00:41:28,092 --> 00:41:32,096
Or a text, or whatever people say that sounds casual and smooth.

837
00:41:32,096 --> 00:41:33,223
[Aza chuckles]

838
00:41:34,641 --> 00:41:35,642
Okay.

839
00:41:36,517 --> 00:41:38,353
[mellow pop music playing]

840
00:41:38,353 --> 00:41:41,064
I should probably go find Daisy.

841
00:41:42,190 --> 00:41:43,399
It's getting late.

842
00:41:45,610 --> 00:41:46,486
Daisy?

843
00:41:50,615 --> 00:41:51,574
[Aza sighs]

844
00:41:56,829 --> 00:41:57,830
Daisy?

845
00:41:58,957 --> 00:41:59,999
Mychal?

846
00:42:01,376 --> 00:42:02,210
[gasps]

847
00:42:03,086 --> 00:42:04,128
[Davis clears throat]

848
00:42:05,296 --> 00:42:06,798
Hey.

849
00:42:06,798 --> 00:42:08,550
Hey, we were just, um...

850
00:42:08,550 --> 00:42:09,842
[Mychal clears throat]

851
00:42:09,842 --> 00:42:10,969
- Uh, we were, we were... - [Mychal] Um...

852
00:42:10,969 --> 00:42:12,553
...we were looking for you!

853
00:42:12,553 --> 00:42:13,846
- Mmm-hmm. Right. - [chuckles]

854
00:42:13,846 --> 00:42:14,889
You weren't in here.

855
00:42:14,889 --> 00:42:16,224
Found us.

856
00:42:16,224 --> 00:42:17,600
[both chuckle]

857
00:42:17,600 --> 00:42:18,768
- You ready to go? - Yeah.

858
00:42:18,768 --> 00:42:19,811
- Great. - Let me see...

859
00:42:19,811 --> 00:42:21,980
[mellow pop music continues]

860
00:42:26,818 --> 00:42:28,695
[lock clicking]

861
00:42:39,580 --> 00:42:43,167
[Gina] Aza, you're home late.

862
00:42:43,167 --> 00:42:46,462
I know. Sorry. Traffic.

863
00:42:50,300 --> 00:42:51,968
[music intensifies]

864
00:42:58,933 --> 00:42:59,767
[gasps]

865
00:43:13,448 --> 00:43:15,199
- [knocking at door] - [gasps]

866
00:43:15,199 --> 00:43:19,412
- I want to hear about your date. - [Aza] I'm changing.

867
00:43:19,412 --> 00:43:21,831
Good night. Talk to you in the morning. I love you.

868
00:43:25,168 --> 00:43:27,086
- [gasps] - [cell phone clicks]

869
00:43:35,595 --> 00:43:36,471
[message whooshes]

870
00:43:41,851 --> 00:43:42,810
[cell phone dings]

871
00:43:46,147 --> 00:43:47,231
[message whooshes]

872
00:43:51,277 --> 00:43:52,153
[cell phone dings]

873
00:43:53,988 --> 00:43:54,906
[message whooshes]

874
00:44:02,830 --> 00:44:03,706
[message whooshes]

875
00:44:06,959 --> 00:44:07,835
[cell phone dings]

876
00:44:11,339 --> 00:44:12,256
[message whooshes]

877
00:44:13,674 --> 00:44:15,218
[exhales]

878
00:44:15,218 --> 00:44:16,928
- [cell phone clicks] - [gasps]

879
00:44:20,515 --> 00:44:21,516
[cell phone dings]

880
00:44:31,734 --> 00:44:33,111
- [laughs] - [music fades]

881
00:44:35,988 --> 00:44:38,741
I'm so glad you're here. Holmsey, I have a lot to say.

882
00:44:38,741 --> 00:44:40,284
Shocker. Listen--

883
00:44:40,284 --> 00:44:42,412
First, I'm sorry to ditch you last night. Very uncool.

884
00:44:42,412 --> 00:44:43,830
That's totally fine. Daisy--

885
00:44:43,830 --> 00:44:45,373
Second, I know, I know, I know.

886
00:44:45,373 --> 00:44:47,041
I convinced you to come so that we could snoop,

887
00:44:47,041 --> 00:44:48,668
and then I didn't even snoop.

888
00:44:48,668 --> 00:44:51,254
But Mychal is kind of amazing.

889
00:44:51,254 --> 00:44:53,506
- And I want to hear all about it, but-- - Mmm, mmm.

890
00:44:53,506 --> 00:44:55,133
The first room that we wandered into

891
00:44:55,133 --> 00:44:58,177
had a fireplace with a remote control.

892
00:44:58,177 --> 00:45:00,721
And then the next thing I know, we were cuddled up on this couch

893
00:45:00,721 --> 00:45:02,765
that was also, like, a literal work of art.

894
00:45:02,765 --> 00:45:06,477
And I don't... One thing led to another and I just let him, I let him--

895
00:45:06,477 --> 00:45:07,645
Daisy!

896
00:45:07,645 --> 00:45:09,647
[upbeat pop music playing on speakers]

897
00:45:12,900 --> 00:45:14,444
Look. Look inside.

898
00:45:14,444 --> 00:45:16,696
Oh, I'm more of a frosted blueberry kind of girl.

899
00:45:16,696 --> 00:45:18,239
Just look inside.

900
00:45:19,407 --> 00:45:20,450
[Daisy scoffs]

901
00:45:21,868 --> 00:45:25,079
[laughs awkwardly]

902
00:45:27,248 --> 00:45:28,124
[exhales]

903
00:45:29,500 --> 00:45:31,419
What in the shit is this?

904
00:45:32,211 --> 00:45:34,005
Davis gave it to me.

905
00:45:34,005 --> 00:45:36,966
I'm not sure if it really makes sense, or if it's ethical. I don't--

906
00:45:36,966 --> 00:45:38,634
Oh, no, no, no.

907
00:45:38,634 --> 00:45:43,764
For once in your life, Aza, do not overthink this.

908
00:45:43,764 --> 00:45:45,183
Oh.

909
00:45:45,183 --> 00:45:47,560
Should I have not come here to tell you you're rich?

910
00:45:47,560 --> 00:45:49,854
It's okay, I can just hold on to your half.

911
00:45:49,854 --> 00:45:50,855
Wait!

912
00:45:52,148 --> 00:45:54,358
Half of this is mine?

913
00:45:54,358 --> 00:45:56,819
There's 50 grand for each of us.

914
00:45:58,362 --> 00:46:01,199
- [music stops] - [Daisy squealing, laughing]

915
00:46:02,867 --> 00:46:04,869
- Oh! Ooh! - [chuckles nervously]

916
00:46:04,869 --> 00:46:07,580
Do you actually think we can keep it?

917
00:46:07,580 --> 00:46:09,999
Hell yes, we can keep it!

918
00:46:09,999 --> 00:46:12,919
Holmsey, I am so done with the idea

919
00:46:12,919 --> 00:46:16,964
that there is, like, nobility in turning down money.

920
00:46:16,964 --> 00:46:19,050
This is gonna change our lives.

921
00:46:19,050 --> 00:46:22,220
I'm actually gonna go to college. Not at night.

922
00:46:22,220 --> 00:46:24,263
Well, it's not enough to pay for all of college.

923
00:46:24,263 --> 00:46:26,807
[sighs] Yeah, I know that, Queen Buzzkill.

924
00:46:26,807 --> 00:46:30,228
But it'll make paying for college a hell of a lot easier.

925
00:46:30,228 --> 00:46:33,356
Aza, you can put this towards Northwestern.

926
00:46:35,733 --> 00:46:37,401
My mom can't know about this money.

927
00:46:37,401 --> 00:46:39,779
Duh, my parents would make me split it with my sister.

928
00:46:39,779 --> 00:46:43,366
No. No. No!

929
00:46:43,366 --> 00:46:49,080
Now, I need you to just accept that the universe

930
00:46:49,080 --> 00:46:51,832
is being awesome with us right now

931
00:46:53,084 --> 00:46:54,585
and lean the eff in.

932
00:46:58,339 --> 00:46:59,840
- I'll try. - Ooh.

933
00:46:59,840 --> 00:47:03,761
Let's go get the finest meal Indianapolis has to offer.

934
00:47:04,971 --> 00:47:06,847
[upbeat country music plays]

935
00:47:06,847 --> 00:47:08,933
[Daisy] Hi, Holly.

936
00:47:08,933 --> 00:47:11,269
Two red wines, please. We're celebrating.

937
00:47:11,269 --> 00:47:13,479
- [Holly] Yeah, nice try. - [clicks tongue] Okay.

938
00:47:13,479 --> 00:47:17,024
Uh, fine, what is your best steak?

939
00:47:17,024 --> 00:47:20,736
The shrimp and parm sirloin is our most popular.

940
00:47:20,736 --> 00:47:22,280
Woof. Okay.

941
00:47:22,280 --> 00:47:25,616
Uh, I will get my usual blazin' Texan burger,

942
00:47:25,616 --> 00:47:28,286
but I would like to upgrade my side to onion rings.

943
00:47:28,286 --> 00:47:30,413
And, yes, I know that it's extra.

944
00:47:30,413 --> 00:47:33,082
- [Holly] And you? - A veggie burger.

945
00:47:33,082 --> 00:47:36,168
Uh, no tomatoes, cheese, mayonnaise, onions--

946
00:47:36,168 --> 00:47:37,628
Yeah, I know your order.

947
00:47:39,338 --> 00:47:42,258
- Do you have a coupon? - [laughing] No.

948
00:47:42,258 --> 00:47:45,344
Not today, Holly. Not today.

949
00:47:46,262 --> 00:47:47,680
[curious music playing]

950
00:47:49,432 --> 00:47:50,725
[typing]

951
00:48:01,193 --> 00:48:02,153
[music fades]

952
00:48:02,153 --> 00:48:03,154
Um...

953
00:48:04,030 --> 00:48:05,364
What is that?

954
00:48:05,364 --> 00:48:07,366
Oh. [chuckles] Isn't he fabulous?

955
00:48:08,242 --> 00:48:09,619
I'm naming him Liam.

956
00:48:11,245 --> 00:48:13,623
I thought we were saving the money for college.

957
00:48:14,457 --> 00:48:16,751
We are. Most of it.

958
00:48:16,751 --> 00:48:20,588
But I talked the car salesman down to $8,400 from $10,000,

959
00:48:20,588 --> 00:48:21,964
so in a way, I made money.

960
00:48:21,964 --> 00:48:24,925
In no way did you make any money.

961
00:48:24,925 --> 00:48:26,844
But you already had a car.

962
00:48:26,844 --> 00:48:29,388
- I'm just saying. - Okay.

963
00:48:32,475 --> 00:48:36,896
Oh. Mychal got into that art show. It's in a couple weeks.

964
00:48:36,896 --> 00:48:39,315
I know that dirty tunnels might be a lot for you,

965
00:48:39,315 --> 00:48:42,443
but is there any way that you could come with me?

966
00:48:43,527 --> 00:48:46,113
Um... Maybe.

967
00:48:47,073 --> 00:48:48,074
[cell phone dings]

968
00:48:53,245 --> 00:48:54,580
Aw.

969
00:48:54,580 --> 00:48:57,291
Mychal wants to meet up before homeroom.

970
00:48:57,291 --> 00:48:59,418
Ugh! I love how into me he is.

971
00:48:59,418 --> 00:49:01,879
["Sunflower Vol. 6" playing]

972
00:49:03,089 --> 00:49:04,048
- [typing] - [message whooshes]

973
00:49:06,634 --> 00:49:07,551
[message whooshes]

974
00:49:12,014 --> 00:49:14,642
Now, how I'mma capture the curve of your face if you keep moving?

975
00:49:14,642 --> 00:49:15,643
Girl, hold still.

976
00:49:15,643 --> 00:49:16,977
You hold still.

977
00:49:16,977 --> 00:49:18,979
[Mychal] That doesn't even make any sense.

978
00:49:22,191 --> 00:49:23,275
[Daisy] Holding still.

979
00:49:26,237 --> 00:49:29,073
♪ Wondering, headshake ♪

980
00:49:29,073 --> 00:49:32,159
♪ Tired eyes Are the death of me... ♪

981
00:49:32,159 --> 00:49:35,037
♪ Mouthful of toothpaste ♪

982
00:49:35,037 --> 00:49:37,915
♪ Before I got to know you ♪

983
00:49:37,915 --> 00:49:41,127
♪ I've got your face ♪

984
00:49:41,127 --> 00:49:43,921
♪ Hung up high In the gallery... ♪

985
00:49:43,921 --> 00:49:45,381
[music soars]

986
00:49:49,343 --> 00:49:50,928
[singer hoots]

987
00:49:53,389 --> 00:49:54,306
[cell phone dings]

988
00:49:58,269 --> 00:49:59,103
[song stops]

989
00:49:59,103 --> 00:50:00,146
- [whistle blows] - [gasps]

990
00:50:00,146 --> 00:50:01,313
Ow!

991
00:50:01,313 --> 00:50:04,442
Aza, can you at least pretend to play?

992
00:50:04,442 --> 00:50:05,943
Sorry, Mr. Adler.

993
00:50:05,943 --> 00:50:07,653
[Adler] All right, everybody, let's get back at it.

994
00:50:07,653 --> 00:50:10,489
♪ My eyes want you more Than a melody... ♪

995
00:50:10,489 --> 00:50:11,574
Mom.

996
00:50:12,533 --> 00:50:15,327
♪ Let me inside ♪

997
00:50:15,327 --> 00:50:18,414
♪ Wish I could Get to know you ♪

998
00:50:19,331 --> 00:50:20,332
Mom?

999
00:50:23,627 --> 00:50:25,504
- [Gina] Stop it. - [man laughs]

1000
00:50:25,504 --> 00:50:28,340
[Gina and man speak indistinctly]

1001
00:50:28,340 --> 00:50:30,676
- [Gina] You're terrible! - [Aza] Hi.

1002
00:50:32,052 --> 00:50:33,053
Hey, honey.

1003
00:50:34,722 --> 00:50:37,475
This is Evan, my friend Debbie's brother.

1004
00:50:38,392 --> 00:50:39,894
Um, you must be Aza.

1005
00:50:39,894 --> 00:50:42,354
I've heard such great things about you.

1006
00:50:42,354 --> 00:50:44,356
Evan is a contractor.

1007
00:50:44,356 --> 00:50:47,193
He came by after work to see if we could turn the garage into a bedroom.

1008
00:50:47,193 --> 00:50:50,863
Why do we need a new bedroom? We never have any guests.

1009
00:50:50,863 --> 00:50:54,033
Actually, I was thinking maybe you'd like it.

1010
00:50:54,033 --> 00:50:58,329
- For the future. - Like, when I'm in college?

1011
00:51:00,581 --> 00:51:02,333
I'll get out of you guys' way.

1012
00:51:02,333 --> 00:51:03,459
[chuckles]

1013
00:51:03,459 --> 00:51:05,294
It's nice to finally meet you, Aza.

1014
00:51:06,420 --> 00:51:08,005
- I'll see you later. - Thank you.

1015
00:51:10,508 --> 00:51:11,509
Bye.

1016
00:51:15,387 --> 00:51:17,973
- You want me to live in the garage? - No.

1017
00:51:17,973 --> 00:51:20,351
I mean, yes, but it wouldn't be a garage.

1018
00:51:20,351 --> 00:51:23,020
We'd make it into more of your own apartment.

1019
00:51:23,729 --> 00:51:24,605
[scoffs]

1020
00:51:26,190 --> 00:51:28,609
So, you think I'm too crazy to live away from home?

1021
00:51:29,735 --> 00:51:31,612
Aza, it was just an idea.

1022
00:51:31,612 --> 00:51:33,447
I was trying to get a sense of the costs.

1023
00:51:33,447 --> 00:51:36,492
And IU is close by and it's such a good school--

1024
00:51:36,492 --> 00:51:37,701
Got it.

1025
00:51:39,495 --> 00:51:42,414
I'm going over to Davis' tonight. He's picking me up.

1026
00:51:44,291 --> 00:51:45,209
[sighs]

1027
00:51:45,209 --> 00:51:46,961
[mellow piano music playing]

1028
00:51:50,297 --> 00:51:54,385
[Aza] I feel like we're back at camp, sitting at the lake.

1029
00:52:01,934 --> 00:52:03,477
You see that bright star up there?

1030
00:52:04,812 --> 00:52:06,355
The one kind of by itself.

1031
00:52:07,606 --> 00:52:09,483
Yeah, I think so.

1032
00:52:12,069 --> 00:52:13,487
Well, that's Tau Ceti.

1033
00:52:14,822 --> 00:52:16,240
It's 12 light-years away,

1034
00:52:16,240 --> 00:52:18,826
and two of its planets might actually be habitable.

1035
00:52:18,826 --> 00:52:21,370
- Well, probably not, but maybe. - [chuckles]

1036
00:52:23,163 --> 00:52:24,456
It's my favorite star.

1037
00:52:26,375 --> 00:52:27,626
Why is that?

1038
00:52:30,045 --> 00:52:31,630
I like to look at it

1039
00:52:31,630 --> 00:52:32,882
and think about how the Sun's light looks to someone

1040
00:52:32,882 --> 00:52:35,092
in Tau Ceti's solar system.

1041
00:52:35,092 --> 00:52:38,178
And right now, they're looking at our light from 12 years ago.

1042
00:52:40,681 --> 00:52:44,852
In the light they see, my mom has five years to live.

1043
00:52:47,271 --> 00:52:49,481
In the light they see, you and I are just kids.

1044
00:52:54,028 --> 00:52:55,946
In the light they see,

1045
00:52:57,948 --> 00:53:01,577
my dad just got me my first tricycle.

1046
00:53:01,577 --> 00:53:06,457
We go to the park, he watches me ride,

1047
00:53:06,457 --> 00:53:10,002
and he tells me I'm going so fast, he can barely see me.

1048
00:53:16,592 --> 00:53:17,593
In the light they see,

1049
00:53:20,387 --> 00:53:22,890
we have the best and worst of it in front of us.

1050
00:53:26,894 --> 00:53:29,605
Hey, how come it's so easy for me to talk to you?

1051
00:53:32,149 --> 00:53:33,400
I don't know.

1052
00:53:36,403 --> 00:53:40,699
Maybe because our hearts are broken in the same places.

1053
00:53:46,246 --> 00:53:47,414
[chuckles]

1054
00:53:53,462 --> 00:53:55,631
You wanna go for a swim?

1055
00:53:55,631 --> 00:53:57,424
[mellow romantic music playing]

1056
00:53:57,424 --> 00:53:59,093
[both chuckle]

1057
00:54:06,058 --> 00:54:08,727
- Turn around. - [chuckles] Okay.

1058
00:54:30,207 --> 00:54:31,709
Okay, you can look now.

1059
00:54:37,923 --> 00:54:38,966
[laughs softly]

1060
00:54:49,226 --> 00:54:50,227
Hi.

1061
00:54:52,271 --> 00:54:54,064
Hi.

1062
00:54:54,064 --> 00:54:56,734
Do you have any big exams or anything Wednesday?

1063
00:54:58,819 --> 00:54:59,820
No.

1064
00:55:00,946 --> 00:55:02,573
Why?

1065
00:55:02,573 --> 00:55:05,576
I was kind of hoping I could convince you to skip school.

1066
00:55:07,161 --> 00:55:08,328
I wanna take you somewhere.

1067
00:55:09,413 --> 00:55:10,414
Where?

1068
00:55:12,833 --> 00:55:14,084
It's a surprise.

1069
00:55:14,084 --> 00:55:15,377
[inhales]

1070
00:55:15,961 --> 00:55:17,087
[laughs]

1071
00:55:17,087 --> 00:55:18,672
[music turns funky]

1072
00:55:20,591 --> 00:55:21,884
[cell phone vibrates]

1073
00:55:32,019 --> 00:55:32,895
[message whooshes]

1074
00:55:44,364 --> 00:55:46,533
[typing]

1075
00:55:48,535 --> 00:55:49,995
[Davis typing]

1076
00:56:20,901 --> 00:56:22,444
[Aza laughing]

1077
00:56:49,513 --> 00:56:50,430
[cell phone clicks]

1078
00:56:51,640 --> 00:56:54,434
[Aza] He's cute and smart.

1079
00:56:54,434 --> 00:56:57,521
And not just because he goes to a fancy prep school.

1080
00:56:57,521 --> 00:56:59,356
[music fades]

1081
00:56:59,356 --> 00:57:02,943
He makes me feel like I'm an acceptable girl to have a crush on.

1082
00:57:02,943 --> 00:57:06,196
And you don't feel that way about yourself?

1083
00:57:06,196 --> 00:57:10,284
I mean, I'm still crazy, if that's what you're asking.

1084
00:57:10,284 --> 00:57:12,953
Nothing's changed on the crazy front.

1085
00:57:12,953 --> 00:57:16,039
I would like to ask you to frame your mental health

1086
00:57:16,039 --> 00:57:18,167
around a word other than "crazy."

1087
00:57:18,167 --> 00:57:20,544
Um, can we try "courageous"?

1088
00:57:20,544 --> 00:57:23,922
[scoffs] God, that's such a psychiatrist move.

1089
00:57:23,922 --> 00:57:26,925
[chuckles] Look, Aza, plenty of people with anxiety disorders

1090
00:57:26,925 --> 00:57:30,053
have fulfilling romantic relationships.

1091
00:57:30,053 --> 00:57:34,099
Yeah, but how can I have a boyfriend if I hate the idea of kissing him?

1092
00:57:34,099 --> 00:57:38,854
Being intimate with another person is intimidating for everyone,

1093
00:57:38,854 --> 00:57:42,482
especially for someone who has already lost someone she loves.

1094
00:57:46,069 --> 00:57:46,987
[exhales]

1095
00:57:47,988 --> 00:57:49,323
What?

1096
00:57:49,323 --> 00:57:51,783
You don't have to make this about my dad dying.

1097
00:57:53,744 --> 00:57:56,246
Not everything is about him.

1098
00:57:56,246 --> 00:58:00,250
But you being afraid to get close to this boy,

1099
00:58:00,250 --> 00:58:02,461
I think it may be a little bit about him.

1100
00:58:06,840 --> 00:58:08,926
He was better with me than my mom.

1101
00:58:10,510 --> 00:58:12,512
He never made me feel broken.

1102
00:58:14,640 --> 00:58:15,849
He'd just tell me,

1103
00:58:18,894 --> 00:58:19,978
"You're right to worry.

1104
00:58:22,648 --> 00:58:24,566
Life is worrisome."

1105
00:58:24,566 --> 00:58:26,777
[melancholy piano music playing]

1106
00:58:29,196 --> 00:58:32,741
Uh, can we go back to talking about the cute boy again, please?

1107
00:58:32,741 --> 00:58:34,451
[inhales sharply]

1108
00:58:36,119 --> 00:58:38,330
Aza, I have to be honest with you.

1109
00:58:38,330 --> 00:58:40,791
I think your progress is being slowed down

1110
00:58:40,791 --> 00:58:45,587
by your refusal to take the medication on a regular basis.

1111
00:58:45,587 --> 00:58:48,298
And I believe your chances of having a relationship

1112
00:58:48,298 --> 00:58:51,927
with this cute, smart boy will be hurt, too.

1113
00:58:53,095 --> 00:58:54,596
I just think it's messed up.

1114
00:58:56,556 --> 00:58:59,393
The idea of having to take a pill to become your real self.

1115
00:58:59,393 --> 00:59:02,729
And here I thought you said you'd kill to have a mind that lets go of things.

1116
00:59:02,729 --> 00:59:06,441
And you said my mind is what makes me, me.

1117
00:59:08,193 --> 00:59:10,028
But it isn't my mind on the pills.

1118
00:59:10,779 --> 00:59:11,947
[Aza scoffs]

1119
00:59:14,616 --> 00:59:16,827
It's a real catch-22, isn't it?

1120
00:59:17,995 --> 00:59:19,162
[doorbell rings]

1121
00:59:19,162 --> 00:59:20,289
[automated voice] Someone's at the door.

1122
00:59:25,210 --> 00:59:26,086
[lock clicks open]

1123
00:59:27,504 --> 00:59:28,797
[Aza] Hey.

1124
00:59:28,797 --> 00:59:31,550
I'm, uh, here to hang out with your brother.

1125
00:59:31,550 --> 00:59:33,010
Oh, yeah, I know.

1126
00:59:33,010 --> 00:59:34,845
He's been trying on different outfits all morning.

1127
00:59:34,845 --> 00:59:37,347
Hey, Davis. Your girlfriend's here.

1128
00:59:37,347 --> 00:59:40,100
- Oh, I'm... I'm not his girlfriend. - Sure.

1129
00:59:41,768 --> 00:59:43,228
Hey, can I show you something?

1130
00:59:43,228 --> 00:59:44,938
[mellow curious music playing]

1131
00:59:44,938 --> 00:59:47,858
Davis said you and your friend were trying to find my dad.

1132
00:59:47,858 --> 00:59:50,569
Oh, uh, no. Not really.

1133
00:59:50,569 --> 00:59:53,780
- Um, I mean, I-- - Uh, just check this out.

1134
00:59:58,285 --> 01:00:01,163
- A permission slip? - Uh, look at the back.

1135
01:00:02,873 --> 01:00:05,208
That's his handwriting.

1136
01:00:05,208 --> 01:00:08,754
[Aza] "Maldives, Kosovo, Cambodia.

1137
01:00:08,754 --> 01:00:10,630
The Joggers Mouth"?

1138
01:00:10,630 --> 01:00:14,259
So, Davis says those first three are countries where people hide out

1139
01:00:14,259 --> 01:00:17,054
when they're wanted in America.

1140
01:00:17,054 --> 01:00:20,057
But I can't figure out The Joggers Mouth.

1141
01:00:20,057 --> 01:00:21,808
Does it mean anything to you?

1142
01:00:23,393 --> 01:00:24,436
Uh...

1143
01:00:24,436 --> 01:00:25,520
No.

1144
01:00:26,772 --> 01:00:28,231
I'm really sorry, Noah.

1145
01:00:28,982 --> 01:00:30,567
[sighs]

1146
01:00:30,567 --> 01:00:31,568
[paper rustles]

1147
01:00:31,568 --> 01:00:33,403
Okay.

1148
01:00:33,403 --> 01:00:35,781
Hi. Sorry to keep you waiting.

1149
01:00:35,781 --> 01:00:37,449
Um, I was thinking really hard about

1150
01:00:37,449 --> 01:00:39,493
whether I can pull this sweater off or not. [chuckles]

1151
01:00:39,493 --> 01:00:41,036
- I like it. - No, you can't.

1152
01:00:42,746 --> 01:00:43,789
You ready to go?

1153
01:00:48,418 --> 01:00:51,838
- You have your own airplane? - No, my father does.

1154
01:00:51,838 --> 01:00:55,050
But my father's not here, so...

1155
01:00:55,050 --> 01:00:59,096
- And you have a full-time pilot, too? - You're looking at him.

1156
01:00:59,096 --> 01:01:01,139
You can get your pilot's license at 16, so...

1157
01:01:02,641 --> 01:01:03,642
Voila.

1158
01:01:06,561 --> 01:01:07,396
Aza, I'm kidding.

1159
01:01:07,396 --> 01:01:08,772
Meet Richard.

1160
01:01:08,772 --> 01:01:09,773
- He's a trained professional. - [both laugh]

1161
01:01:09,773 --> 01:01:10,941
[laughing] Oh.

1162
01:01:10,941 --> 01:01:12,776
- [Davis] Hi. - [Aza] Hi.

1163
01:01:13,443 --> 01:01:14,236
[Aza exhales]

1164
01:01:14,236 --> 01:01:16,405
["Zeitgeist" playing]

1165
01:01:18,365 --> 01:01:20,117
- [Aza typing] - [Davis] Howdy, Richard.

1166
01:01:20,117 --> 01:01:22,953
♪ Outside lies within... ♪

1167
01:01:22,953 --> 01:01:24,830
[message whooshes]

1168
01:01:24,830 --> 01:01:28,208
- You coming? Come on. - [Aza laughs]

1169
01:01:28,208 --> 01:01:29,709
♪ Soft spring winds Have blown... ♪

1170
01:01:29,709 --> 01:01:31,711
[laughing] Oh. What!

1171
01:01:31,711 --> 01:01:33,713
♪ Let the note fly ♪

1172
01:01:33,713 --> 01:01:35,340
♪ East-West ♪

1173
01:01:35,340 --> 01:01:37,968
[vocalizing]

1174
01:01:39,219 --> 01:01:40,470
[imperceptible]

1175
01:01:44,558 --> 01:01:48,687
♪ I never dreamed it could be like this ♪

1176
01:01:48,687 --> 01:01:50,730
♪ Takes a little time ♪

1177
01:01:50,730 --> 01:01:52,899
♪ Let the note fly ♪

1178
01:01:54,067 --> 01:01:57,654
♪ Hard time mantle of time... ♪

1179
01:01:57,654 --> 01:01:59,239
[Richard] Lady and gentleman,

1180
01:01:59,239 --> 01:02:01,783
we will be touching down in Chicago shortly.

1181
01:02:01,783 --> 01:02:02,993
Chicago?

1182
01:02:02,993 --> 01:02:05,829
Richard, it was supposed to be a surprise.

1183
01:02:05,829 --> 01:02:07,789
What are we doing in Chicago?

1184
01:02:09,082 --> 01:02:11,543
- It's still a surprise. - [laughs]

1185
01:02:11,543 --> 01:02:15,297
♪ Honey, honey, I know ♪

1186
01:02:15,297 --> 01:02:17,340
♪ For what it's worth... ♪

1187
01:02:17,340 --> 01:02:19,176
[Davis] You said you dreamed of going to Northwestern,

1188
01:02:19,176 --> 01:02:21,511
but that new things aren't really your strong suit.

1189
01:02:21,511 --> 01:02:25,640
So, I thought if you saw the school, it wouldn't be new anymore.

1190
01:02:26,433 --> 01:02:27,893
It'd be old-new.

1191
01:02:30,437 --> 01:02:32,230
Are we going on a campus tour?

1192
01:02:34,816 --> 01:02:36,568
Not exactly.

1193
01:02:36,568 --> 01:02:39,863
♪ Outside lies within ♪

1194
01:02:39,863 --> 01:02:41,948
♪ Peel the mandarin ♪

1195
01:02:41,948 --> 01:02:45,035
♪ Love has just begun ♪

1196
01:02:45,035 --> 01:02:47,829
♪ Somewhere In space and time... ♪

1197
01:02:47,829 --> 01:02:48,872
[softly] Come on.

1198
01:02:48,872 --> 01:02:50,582
♪ Like a bank heist ♪

1199
01:02:50,582 --> 01:02:52,709
♪ It's the Zeitgeist ♪

1200
01:02:52,709 --> 01:02:54,085
[song fades]

1201
01:02:55,837 --> 01:02:57,255
[Aza chuckles]

1202
01:02:57,255 --> 01:03:00,133
I can't believe Professor Abbott is right there.

1203
01:03:00,133 --> 01:03:02,427
Okay.

1204
01:03:02,427 --> 01:03:05,889
We have 90 minutes. Let's crack free will, shall we?

1205
01:03:05,889 --> 01:03:07,807
[students laughing]

1206
01:03:07,807 --> 01:03:09,893
You, eating Doritos.

1207
01:03:10,894 --> 01:03:11,895
Why?

1208
01:03:13,146 --> 01:03:14,397
Why?

1209
01:03:14,397 --> 01:03:16,066
[Professor Abbott] Is it because you were hungry

1210
01:03:16,066 --> 01:03:17,901
and you made that choice?

1211
01:03:17,901 --> 01:03:20,487
Is it because advertising works?

1212
01:03:20,487 --> 01:03:22,906
[students laughing]

1213
01:03:22,906 --> 01:03:25,617
Is it because there was only ever going to be one world,

1214
01:03:25,617 --> 01:03:27,786
and everything was predetermined from the start,

1215
01:03:27,786 --> 01:03:29,996
and you were always gonna take this class?

1216
01:03:29,996 --> 01:03:32,249
Well, in our reading, there was that part

1217
01:03:32,249 --> 01:03:34,626
where Kant said, "Freedom of the will must be presupposed."

1218
01:03:34,626 --> 01:03:39,172
So, I suppose I could've chosen any food, but I chose Doritos.

1219
01:03:39,172 --> 01:03:41,091
You did the reading. Wonderful news.

1220
01:03:41,091 --> 01:03:43,260
[students laughing]

1221
01:03:43,260 --> 01:03:47,931
And if we just assume that Kant was right, maybe we don't need the full 90 minutes.

1222
01:03:47,931 --> 01:03:51,059
But Kant was writing about this in the 18th century, right?

1223
01:03:51,059 --> 01:03:54,980
And now we have all these fancy brain contraptions.

1224
01:03:54,980 --> 01:03:59,067
In 2008, volunteers were asked to choose to push a button

1225
01:03:59,067 --> 01:04:02,571
with either their right hand or their left hand.

1226
01:04:02,571 --> 01:04:07,200
And the researchers found that they could predict with 100% accuracy

1227
01:04:07,200 --> 01:04:09,035
which choice the people would make

1228
01:04:09,035 --> 01:04:11,288
seven seconds before the person believed they'd made it.

1229
01:04:11,288 --> 01:04:13,665
[mellow music playing]

1230
01:04:13,665 --> 01:04:16,084
So, I ask you again.

1231
01:04:16,084 --> 01:04:18,795
When did you decide to eat Doritos?

1232
01:04:18,795 --> 01:04:20,630
And who made that choice?

1233
01:04:21,673 --> 01:04:22,841
I have no idea.

1234
01:04:22,841 --> 01:04:24,801
[students laughing]

1235
01:04:24,801 --> 01:04:26,803
[Professor Abbott] It's difficult to define these terms.

1236
01:04:26,803 --> 01:04:28,888
- I need to take this class. - [Professor Abbott] What constitutes

1237
01:04:28,888 --> 01:04:31,891
freedom of choice in a world where our choices are endlessly limited

1238
01:04:31,891 --> 01:04:33,977
and shaped by cultural forces?

1239
01:04:33,977 --> 01:04:37,564
I mean, she has a way of taking these old, dusty philosophical questions

1240
01:04:37,564 --> 01:04:40,191
and making them feel so insanely relevant.

1241
01:04:40,191 --> 01:04:41,443
Oh, my God. Oh, my God.

1242
01:04:45,363 --> 01:04:46,364
Okay.

1243
01:04:49,326 --> 01:04:50,368
Why are we hiding?

1244
01:04:53,163 --> 01:04:54,664
Because she's right there.

1245
01:04:55,999 --> 01:04:57,500
[Davis] You should go talk to her.

1246
01:04:57,500 --> 01:04:59,377
- What would I even say? - Aza.

1247
01:05:00,503 --> 01:05:01,588
You got this.

1248
01:05:11,931 --> 01:05:13,516
[Aza] Hi, Professor Abbott.

1249
01:05:15,101 --> 01:05:17,479
I wanted to introduce myself.

1250
01:05:17,479 --> 01:05:19,648
I'm Aza Holmes, and I'm...

1251
01:05:21,858 --> 01:05:23,151
a fan.

1252
01:05:23,151 --> 01:05:25,654
Are you a fan of being my student?

1253
01:05:25,654 --> 01:05:29,032
No, I'm still in high school. I'm just visiting today.

1254
01:05:30,283 --> 01:05:31,993
But you're the reason I wanna go here.

1255
01:05:34,204 --> 01:05:37,540
I just think you're the most brilliant person in the world.

1256
01:05:37,540 --> 01:05:39,376
Oh, well. [chuckles]

1257
01:05:39,376 --> 01:05:43,004
Thank you so much, but I assure you that's not the case.

1258
01:05:43,797 --> 01:05:44,673
[chuckles]

1259
01:05:47,467 --> 01:05:48,593
It was nice to meet you.

1260
01:05:51,971 --> 01:05:53,014
Professor Abbott?

1261
01:05:54,099 --> 01:05:55,100
Yes?

1262
01:05:59,604 --> 01:06:01,398
I'm worried that I'm not real.

1263
01:06:02,440 --> 01:06:03,608
[music fades]

1264
01:06:06,653 --> 01:06:08,238
Why is that?

1265
01:06:08,238 --> 01:06:10,198
It's like what you said.

1266
01:06:10,198 --> 01:06:14,202
A lot of my thoughts and behaviors don't even feel like they're mine.

1267
01:06:15,662 --> 01:06:20,417
Like, they're not things I want to think or do.

1268
01:06:20,417 --> 01:06:24,170
And then when I look for the originator of my thoughts, the real me,

1269
01:06:25,213 --> 01:06:26,423
I never find it.

1270
01:06:27,924 --> 01:06:30,260
You know those nesting dolls?

1271
01:06:30,260 --> 01:06:32,095
The ones that are hollow and when you open them up,

1272
01:06:32,095 --> 01:06:33,972
there's a smaller one inside?

1273
01:06:33,972 --> 01:06:37,100
And you keep opening them until eventually you get to the smallest one,

1274
01:06:37,100 --> 01:06:38,852
and it's solid all the way through?

1275
01:06:40,311 --> 01:06:43,398
But with me, I don't think there's one that's solid.

1276
01:06:43,398 --> 01:06:46,818
I just keep getting smaller and smaller.

1277
01:06:52,490 --> 01:06:54,284
That reminds me of an old story.

1278
01:06:54,868 --> 01:06:55,702
Um...

1279
01:06:59,080 --> 01:07:00,790
There's this very famous scientist,

1280
01:07:00,790 --> 01:07:05,128
and he's giving a lecture on astronomy to a large audience.

1281
01:07:05,128 --> 01:07:08,423
And he's describing how the Earth orbits the Sun,

1282
01:07:08,423 --> 01:07:11,718
and the Sun orbits the galaxy, etcetera, etcetera.

1283
01:07:11,718 --> 01:07:13,344
And when he's finally done,

1284
01:07:13,344 --> 01:07:15,805
a little old lady in the back raises her hand and says,

1285
01:07:15,805 --> 01:07:19,392
"Professor, with all due respect, what you've just told us is bullshit.

1286
01:07:19,392 --> 01:07:24,314
The truth is, the Earth is resting on the back of a giant turtle."

1287
01:07:24,314 --> 01:07:27,609
So, the very famous scientist gives a smug smile and says,

1288
01:07:27,609 --> 01:07:30,361
"So, what is the turtle resting on?"

1289
01:07:30,361 --> 01:07:32,655
And the woman replies,

1290
01:07:32,655 --> 01:07:36,701
"It's resting on the shell of another giant turtle, of course."

1291
01:07:36,701 --> 01:07:39,454
And the scientist says, "And that turtle?"

1292
01:07:39,454 --> 01:07:42,499
And the woman exclaims, "Don't you understand?

1293
01:07:42,499 --> 01:07:44,876
It's turtles, all the way down."

1294
01:07:48,296 --> 01:07:50,298
You're imagining the true self,

1295
01:07:50,298 --> 01:07:54,427
the self that exists independent of circumstance,

1296
01:07:54,427 --> 01:07:57,847
as that turtle on the very bottom of the pile.

1297
01:07:57,847 --> 01:08:02,811
But maybe it's turtles, all the way down.

1298
01:08:04,729 --> 01:08:06,022
Maybe you're infinite.

1299
01:08:06,022 --> 01:08:07,816
[upbeat pop music building]

1300
01:08:07,816 --> 01:08:13,530
And I think your doubts make you more real, not less.

1301
01:08:17,867 --> 01:08:19,702
I think you'd do just fine here, Aza.

1302
01:08:26,543 --> 01:08:28,086
[laughs]

1303
01:08:30,922 --> 01:08:32,549
[laughing]

1304
01:08:34,259 --> 01:08:35,385
So, how'd it go?

1305
01:08:41,599 --> 01:08:42,559
Ah.

1306
01:08:43,393 --> 01:08:44,727
[both chuckle]

1307
01:08:51,818 --> 01:08:55,321
[Aza] His bacteria are joining your bacteria right now.

1308
01:08:56,197 --> 01:08:57,907
It doesn't matter.

1309
01:08:57,907 --> 01:08:59,617
People kiss all the time.

1310
01:09:01,661 --> 01:09:04,873
[tense music playing]

1311
01:09:04,873 --> 01:09:08,918
Millions of his organisms are entering your body right now.

1312
01:09:11,045 --> 01:09:12,046
[softly] Hey.

1313
01:09:13,006 --> 01:09:14,132
Is everything okay?

1314
01:09:15,341 --> 01:09:16,634
[Aza] Hmm.

1315
01:09:16,634 --> 01:09:17,927
Yeah, I'm just, uh...

1316
01:09:20,180 --> 01:09:21,598
Just a little nauseous.

1317
01:09:21,598 --> 01:09:22,849
Be right back.

1318
01:09:26,185 --> 01:09:30,023
[door opens, closes]

1319
01:09:30,023 --> 01:09:31,608
- What were you thinking? - [panting]

1320
01:09:31,608 --> 01:09:33,651
Everything was going perfectly and you had to ruin it

1321
01:09:33,651 --> 01:09:35,987
by letting his tongue into your mouth.

1322
01:09:37,363 --> 01:09:38,990
Why isn't it working?

1323
01:09:38,990 --> 01:09:41,075
- [static crackles] - [water running]

1324
01:09:41,075 --> 01:09:43,411
[panting]

1325
01:09:43,411 --> 01:09:44,621
You're infected.

1326
01:09:47,332 --> 01:09:49,751
You need to kill the bacteria now, now, now.

1327
01:09:50,793 --> 01:09:51,794
Oh, shut up!

1328
01:09:52,962 --> 01:09:55,673
Kill it. Kill every last germ.

1329
01:09:55,673 --> 01:09:57,175
Kill his saliva.

1330
01:09:59,052 --> 01:10:02,388
You know what you need to do, so do it. Just do it.

1331
01:10:06,225 --> 01:10:07,644
[music fades]

1332
01:10:12,607 --> 01:10:13,858
[breathing heavily]

1333
01:10:29,332 --> 01:10:30,750
Aza, what's goin' on?

1334
01:10:42,011 --> 01:10:44,389
When I get stuck in my thoughts,

1335
01:10:46,557 --> 01:10:49,978
it's not just the interesting fish stuff I told you about.

1336
01:10:52,397 --> 01:10:53,398
Okay.

1337
01:10:59,362 --> 01:11:01,698
Bacteria scares me more than anything.

1338
01:11:03,408 --> 01:11:05,660
I'm always worried about getting infections,

1339
01:11:05,660 --> 01:11:07,954
and once I start worrying, I can't stop.

1340
01:11:11,624 --> 01:11:15,253
That's why I liked your painting so much.

1341
01:11:15,253 --> 01:11:17,130
Because my thoughts are like that.

1342
01:11:18,631 --> 01:11:23,219
They're all, like, spirally.

1343
01:11:23,219 --> 01:11:26,222
So, right now you're thinking about whether you have an infection?

1344
01:11:27,181 --> 01:11:28,433
Kind of.

1345
01:11:33,938 --> 01:11:35,106
From kissing you.

1346
01:11:37,066 --> 01:11:39,152
[somber piano music playing]

1347
01:11:42,196 --> 01:11:45,491
We don't have to kiss. We can just take things really slow.

1348
01:11:45,491 --> 01:11:46,909
You don't understand.

1349
01:11:47,910 --> 01:11:49,203
It's not logical.

1350
01:11:51,372 --> 01:11:54,125
It's like I'm living with a demon.

1351
01:11:54,125 --> 01:11:57,795
And maybe you don't care right now, but you won't feel that way forever.

1352
01:11:59,672 --> 01:12:00,798
But it's not forever.

1353
01:12:01,924 --> 01:12:02,967
It's now.

1354
01:12:10,349 --> 01:12:11,267
[lock clicks open]

1355
01:12:14,562 --> 01:12:16,189
Oh, thank God.

1356
01:12:16,189 --> 01:12:17,482
- Where have you been? - [door shuts]

1357
01:12:17,482 --> 01:12:18,816
[lock clicks]

1358
01:12:18,816 --> 01:12:20,568
Why haven't you been answering your phone?

1359
01:12:20,568 --> 01:12:22,028
[exhales shakily]

1360
01:12:23,613 --> 01:12:24,989
I was with Davis.

1361
01:12:27,700 --> 01:12:30,953
I see you at school at the same time every single day, Aza.

1362
01:12:30,953 --> 01:12:33,122
Did you seriously think I wasn't gonna find out?

1363
01:12:33,122 --> 01:12:36,250
I don't know. I guess I didn't care.

1364
01:12:36,250 --> 01:12:39,712
- Well, where'd you guys go? - We went to Chicago.

1365
01:12:39,712 --> 01:12:41,130
To Chicago?

1366
01:12:41,130 --> 01:12:43,633
What the hell were you doing in Chicago?

1367
01:12:43,633 --> 01:12:45,093
He didn't kidnap me. I wanted to go.

1368
01:12:45,093 --> 01:12:47,428
Well, I don't care if you wanted to go.

1369
01:12:47,428 --> 01:12:51,349
What if something happened to you and I didn't even know where you were?

1370
01:12:51,349 --> 01:12:54,602
Or what if you got in your head and you needed me to calm you down?

1371
01:12:54,602 --> 01:12:56,437
You think you calm me down?

1372
01:12:57,063 --> 01:12:59,107
How, Mom?

1373
01:12:59,107 --> 01:13:01,984
Do you know why Davis took me to Chicago today?

1374
01:13:01,984 --> 01:13:03,236
[breathes shakily]

1375
01:13:03,236 --> 01:13:05,530
To see Northwestern.

1376
01:13:05,530 --> 01:13:08,366
Because he actually thinks that I can go to school on my own

1377
01:13:08,366 --> 01:13:09,951
in another state.

1378
01:13:09,951 --> 01:13:12,036
He thinks that I can study what I wanna study,

1379
01:13:12,036 --> 01:13:13,955
and live in a dorm with the other kids.

1380
01:13:13,955 --> 01:13:17,625
And maybe he's wrong. Maybe I can't.

1381
01:13:17,625 --> 01:13:21,295
But do you know how good it feels to finally have someone in my life

1382
01:13:21,295 --> 01:13:22,755
who thinks I can?

1383
01:13:24,132 --> 01:13:25,299
- Aza! - [door slams]

1384
01:13:27,760 --> 01:13:29,887
[somber instrumental music playing]

1385
01:13:33,891 --> 01:13:35,101
[cell phone dings]

1386
01:13:56,789 --> 01:13:57,665
[clicks mouse]

1387
01:13:58,666 --> 01:14:01,377
[typing]

1388
01:14:08,009 --> 01:14:12,638
[Daisy] "Chewie and I were on Tatooine where we'd stopped to pick up some cargo.

1389
01:14:12,638 --> 01:14:19,437
Of course, Ayala was with me. My best friend and my greatest burden."

1390
01:14:23,816 --> 01:14:24,984
[clicking mouse]

1391
01:14:27,570 --> 01:14:31,115
"Ayala reached into her mane of blue hair and plucked out a strand.

1392
01:14:31,115 --> 01:14:35,244
A nervous habit, but then, all her habits were nervous."

1393
01:14:40,958 --> 01:14:43,252
"Ayala never got anything right.

1394
01:14:43,252 --> 01:14:46,547
The more Ayala worried, the worse she made everything."

1395
01:14:49,675 --> 01:14:52,553
"Ayala sneered at my new Speeder Bike.

1396
01:14:52,553 --> 01:14:54,889
'I thought you were saving your money.'

1397
01:14:54,889 --> 01:15:00,269
Ayala had a Landspeeder from her parents. What did she know?

1398
01:15:00,269 --> 01:15:03,856
Ayala wasn't a bad person. Just a useless one."

1399
01:15:03,856 --> 01:15:05,524
[music crescendoes, fades]

1400
01:15:06,692 --> 01:15:07,568
[gasps]

1401
01:15:07,568 --> 01:15:09,737
[intense pop music playing]

1402
01:15:10,947 --> 01:15:12,198
[cell phone vibrates]

1403
01:15:25,378 --> 01:15:27,171
- [locker clicks open] - [cell phone vibrating]

1404
01:15:36,722 --> 01:15:38,849
[laughing, speaking indistinctly]

1405
01:15:45,606 --> 01:15:46,774
[cell phone vibrates]

1406
01:15:58,703 --> 01:16:00,162
- [music stops] - [Daisy] Holmsey!

1407
01:16:00,162 --> 01:16:02,081
Where have you been all day?

1408
01:16:02,081 --> 01:16:04,208
Can I ride with you to Mychal's later? My mom had to borrow my car.

1409
01:16:04,208 --> 01:16:05,835
And I need the deets on your date.

1410
01:16:07,586 --> 01:16:09,338
I just don't get it.

1411
01:16:09,338 --> 01:16:10,965
He whisks you away to Chicago.

1412
01:16:10,965 --> 01:16:14,176
You have the most romantic day in the history of ever.

1413
01:16:14,176 --> 01:16:16,512
[chuckles] Now you don't want to see him anymore?

1414
01:16:16,512 --> 01:16:18,514
Something else must've happened.

1415
01:16:20,641 --> 01:16:21,684
Did you guys do it?

1416
01:16:21,684 --> 01:16:24,103
No. I told you nothing happened.

1417
01:16:24,103 --> 01:16:26,814
Yeah, but that doesn't make any sense. I'm sure that--

1418
01:16:26,814 --> 01:16:28,566
I screwed it up?

1419
01:16:28,566 --> 01:16:29,692
[chuckles] What?

1420
01:16:30,693 --> 01:16:31,652
No.

1421
01:16:33,237 --> 01:16:35,573
You barely even changed my name.

1422
01:16:40,453 --> 01:16:41,537
You read my fanfic?

1423
01:16:41,537 --> 01:16:43,581
Yes, I read your fanfic!

1424
01:16:43,581 --> 01:16:46,917
You're my best friend. You didn't think maybe I'd read it?

1425
01:16:48,753 --> 01:16:50,921
No, I didn't.

1426
01:16:52,423 --> 01:16:54,175
Because I've been writing them since I was 11,

1427
01:16:54,175 --> 01:16:55,885
and you've never read a single one.

1428
01:16:55,885 --> 01:16:58,262
What? You never asked.

1429
01:16:58,262 --> 01:17:00,639
I shouldn't have to ask.

1430
01:17:00,639 --> 01:17:03,142
Other people's best friends would just

1431
01:17:03,142 --> 01:17:05,353
take a little break from endlessly contemplating

1432
01:17:05,353 --> 01:17:10,066
the inner workings of their own mind to think about other people's interests.

1433
01:17:10,066 --> 01:17:11,942
I came up with Ayala in seventh grade.

1434
01:17:11,942 --> 01:17:15,905
And, yeah, it was a dick move,

1435
01:17:15,905 --> 01:17:18,908
but maybe it was my way of coping with having a best friend who's...

1436
01:17:22,662 --> 01:17:26,290
Look, Mychal says that you're like mustard, right?

1437
01:17:26,290 --> 01:17:31,879
Like, you're awesome in small quantities, but a lot of you is a lot.

1438
01:17:33,005 --> 01:17:34,757
Got it.

1439
01:17:34,757 --> 01:17:37,593
I mean, have you ever stopped to think about what it's like for me?

1440
01:17:37,593 --> 01:17:40,304
I constantly have your back and you never have mine.

1441
01:17:40,304 --> 01:17:41,597
You're right.

1442
01:17:41,597 --> 01:17:42,765
All our friendship has gotten you lately

1443
01:17:42,765 --> 01:17:44,558
is 50 grand and a boyfriend.

1444
01:17:44,558 --> 01:17:47,103
But you won't even come with me to his art show.

1445
01:17:47,103 --> 01:17:48,562
Other people's best friends would...

1446
01:17:49,563 --> 01:17:51,107
[breathing heavily]

1447
01:17:51,107 --> 01:17:53,150
I mean, I know that you have mental problems and whatever,

1448
01:17:53,150 --> 01:17:55,277
but they make you extremely self-centered.

1449
01:17:55,277 --> 01:17:56,821
I am not self-centered!

1450
01:17:56,821 --> 01:17:58,322
What are my parents' names?

1451
01:18:00,866 --> 01:18:02,243
What are their jobs?

1452
01:18:03,619 --> 01:18:04,787
What's my middle name?

1453
01:18:04,787 --> 01:18:06,080
Do I have any pets?

1454
01:18:09,583 --> 01:18:14,422
You're just so pathologically uncurious

1455
01:18:14,422 --> 01:18:16,382
that you don't even know what you don't know.

1456
01:18:18,134 --> 01:18:19,844
- You have a cat. - Yes.

1457
01:18:19,844 --> 01:18:22,138
Congratulations, Aza, I have a cat.

1458
01:18:22,138 --> 01:18:24,473
I've had her since I was five. What's her name?

1459
01:18:27,017 --> 01:18:28,978
- I don't know. - No, you have no clue.

1460
01:18:28,978 --> 01:18:33,357
You have no idea what it's like for me, because you never ask.

1461
01:18:33,357 --> 01:18:35,651
I mean, you think you and your mom are poor or whatever,

1462
01:18:35,651 --> 01:18:39,613
but you got braces, and a car, and an iPad, and all that shit.

1463
01:18:39,613 --> 01:18:40,990
Do you think that it's just normal

1464
01:18:40,990 --> 01:18:42,491
to have a house with your own room in it?

1465
01:18:42,491 --> 01:18:44,160
And a mom who helps you with your homework?

1466
01:18:44,160 --> 01:18:47,538
I mean, your privileges are like oxygen to you, Aza.

1467
01:18:47,538 --> 01:18:51,041
And then you judge me for buying a car instead of saving it all for college.

1468
01:18:51,041 --> 01:18:55,171
And I try not to care, and I try to be understanding because--

1469
01:18:55,171 --> 01:18:56,547
Stop talking!

1470
01:18:56,547 --> 01:18:59,341
Jesus Christ, you haven't shut up in ten years!

1471
01:18:59,341 --> 01:19:01,719
I'm sorry if it's hard hanging out with me,

1472
01:19:01,719 --> 01:19:03,262
but imagine actually being me!

1473
01:19:03,262 --> 01:19:05,347
Imagine being stuck in my head all the time

1474
01:19:05,347 --> 01:19:06,974
with no break, no way out!

1475
01:19:06,974 --> 01:19:08,350
I'm sorry that--

1476
01:19:08,350 --> 01:19:09,852
And to use Mychal's clever little analogy.

1477
01:19:09,852 --> 01:19:12,271
Imagine eating nothing but mustard!

1478
01:19:12,271 --> 01:19:14,273
Being stuck with mustard all the time!

1479
01:19:14,273 --> 01:19:16,275
Okay, I shouldn't have said that.

1480
01:19:16,275 --> 01:19:18,110
And if you hate me so much then why don't you just stop asking me--

1481
01:19:18,110 --> 01:19:19,445
Watch out!

1482
01:19:19,445 --> 01:19:21,238
- [tense music playing] - [tires screeching]

1483
01:19:23,991 --> 01:19:25,034
[music fades]

1484
01:19:30,164 --> 01:19:32,291
[somber tense music playing]

1485
01:19:50,309 --> 01:19:51,519
[echoing] Are you okay?

1486
01:19:52,978 --> 01:19:53,938
[Aza] I think so.

1487
01:19:53,938 --> 01:19:55,564
[breathing heavily]

1488
01:19:56,774 --> 01:19:57,900
Are you?

1489
01:19:58,651 --> 01:19:59,652
You're hurt.

1490
01:20:01,529 --> 01:20:05,157
Aza, stop.

1491
01:20:05,157 --> 01:20:07,076
- [Aza grunts] - Stay still.

1492
01:20:08,202 --> 01:20:09,036
Aza!

1493
01:20:10,538 --> 01:20:12,039
[glass crunching]

1494
01:20:13,707 --> 01:20:18,087
No, no, no.

1495
01:20:19,547 --> 01:20:20,714
[crying] No.

1496
01:20:31,600 --> 01:20:33,894
[firefighter] Hey. Are you all right?

1497
01:20:33,894 --> 01:20:35,020
It's ruined.

1498
01:20:36,105 --> 01:20:37,606
My dad's car.

1499
01:20:37,606 --> 01:20:39,817
[firefighter] Honey, I've been doing this for a long time,

1500
01:20:39,817 --> 01:20:42,778
and I can promise, your dad's not gonna be mad at you.

1501
01:20:42,778 --> 01:20:44,280
[Aza breathing heavily]

1502
01:20:49,827 --> 01:20:51,745
I need help!

1503
01:20:51,745 --> 01:20:53,914
[somber tense music continues]

1504
01:21:02,715 --> 01:21:03,757
[music fades]

1505
01:21:06,719 --> 01:21:07,761
Hey, kiddo.

1506
01:21:09,597 --> 01:21:12,558
You're in the hospital, but everything's gonna be okay.

1507
01:21:14,435 --> 01:21:15,894
I love you so much.

1508
01:21:17,688 --> 01:21:20,774
Mmm, is... Is Daisy okay?

1509
01:21:20,774 --> 01:21:23,152
They gave her some stitches on her forehead,

1510
01:21:23,152 --> 01:21:24,695
but she's gonna be fine.

1511
01:21:26,196 --> 01:21:28,240
[sobbing]

1512
01:21:29,033 --> 01:21:31,118
I ruined Harold.

1513
01:21:31,118 --> 01:21:33,329
I know, baby.

1514
01:21:33,329 --> 01:21:35,164
Dad loved that car.

1515
01:21:36,624 --> 01:21:38,000
Dad loved you.

1516
01:21:39,627 --> 01:21:41,462
Am I okay?

1517
01:21:41,462 --> 01:21:43,172
You have a lacerated liver,

1518
01:21:43,172 --> 01:21:45,466
but the internal bleeding is mild.

1519
01:21:45,466 --> 01:21:47,676
- I'm bleeding inside? - Just a little.

1520
01:21:47,676 --> 01:21:49,011
[breathing heavily]

1521
01:21:49,011 --> 01:21:50,554
The good news is you don't need surgery.

1522
01:21:50,554 --> 01:21:51,889
- They just wanna monitor you... - [sobs]

1523
01:21:51,889 --> 01:21:53,140
...for a few days.

1524
01:21:53,140 --> 01:21:54,141
What?

1525
01:21:55,142 --> 01:21:57,102
No. [cries]

1526
01:21:57,102 --> 01:21:59,563
- No, I can't stay here, Mom. - Aza.

1527
01:21:59,563 --> 01:22:03,108
Please, please just do something. I can't stay here.

1528
01:22:03,108 --> 01:22:04,485
[gasping]

1529
01:22:04,485 --> 01:22:06,195
I have to go home.

1530
01:22:06,195 --> 01:22:08,697
I'm gonna get C. diff if I stay.

1531
01:22:08,697 --> 01:22:10,407
- You don't have C. diff. - I probably

1532
01:22:10,407 --> 01:22:11,700
already have C. diff.

1533
01:22:11,700 --> 01:22:13,702
No, you don't have C. diff.

1534
01:22:13,702 --> 01:22:16,288
Hospitals are where people get it, okay?

1535
01:22:16,288 --> 01:22:18,040
I can't stay here, Mom.

1536
01:22:18,040 --> 01:22:20,209
- I can't stay here! - Aza! Aza!

1537
01:22:20,209 --> 01:22:22,920
- Your liver is bleeding! We can't go home. - [crying] I have...

1538
01:22:22,920 --> 01:22:24,963
But I'll be with you the whole time.

1539
01:22:24,963 --> 01:22:27,591
- I promise. - I have to go. I have to go--

1540
01:22:27,591 --> 01:22:29,802
[tense pulsating music playing]

1541
